	<p>
		I'm James Maddock. I've been a Remedial Massage Therapist since 2007 — first in
		Aotearoa New Zealand, and in Melbourne since 2012 — and in 2026 I completed a
		Bachelor of Health Science (Clinical Myotherapy) at Torrens University, undertaken
		part-time over six years while continuing to practise. Along the way I've taught
		anatomy and massage technique at diploma level, and I plan to return to teaching.
	</p>

	<h2>Why I practise the way I do</h2>
	<p>
		I hold a holistic position that's accountable to current evidence. The nervous system
		is the central organising principle of my practice: it governs muscle function,
		movement, posture, and how you experience all of it. Whatever the intervention —
		hands-on work, dry needling, movement, or education — it is ultimately processed by
		your nervous system, which decides what happens next.
	</p>
	<p>
		Because of that, I understand my role as a <strong>facilitator of healing</strong>,
		not the healer. I can't reach inside your cells and make them work — everything I
		do is, to some degree, indirect. What I can do is create the optimal sensory,
		relational, and contextual conditions for your own healing processes to operate at
		their best. Your autonomy and self-efficacy are explicit goals in every case.
	</p>

	<h2>Contact at the boundary</h2>
	<p>
		The principle that governs my practice I call <em>contact at the boundary</em>: meeting
		you precisely where you are — physiologically and emotionally — and moving forward
		at the rate your system is genuinely ready to move. Holistic practice isn't a
		philosophical aspiration for me; it's a present-moment discipline applied in every
		session.
	</p>

	<h2>Mind and body are one system</h2>
	<p>
		I hold a non-dualist position: the mind is the body and the body is the mind. Every
		treatment integrates your cognitive and emotional state, your beliefs about your
		condition, your social context, and your story about injury and recovery — alongside
		the direct manual and movement interventions. Technique is always in the service of
		the whole person.
	</p>

	<h2>Where I practise</h2>
